{
  "$comment": "The country list the checkout's delivery-address selection offers, and the EU membership flag the shipping zone is priced from. MOCK DATA, ON THE SAME FOOTING AS catalogue.json: Task 5 replaces it with Medusa regions, which is why it lives here and not in content/ — it is data a data layer supersedes, not copy. 'code' is ISO 3166-1 alpha-2, carried so Task 5 can map each entry onto a Medusa region; the form's value is 'name', because the field keeps autoComplete='country-name' and a browser fills a country NAME into it. The list is the 249 officially assigned ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 entries, so it offers every country: content/legal/shipping.ts says 'We ship to every country.', and restricting where the site ships is a commercial decision the operator has not made. Names are Node's own ICU English region names (Intl.DisplayNames), sorted by name. 'euMember' MEANS ONE OF THE 27 EU MEMBER STATES AND NOTHING ELSE. It is the load-bearing field in this file: it selects between the two operator-frozen shipping rates in shipping.json, so a wrong flag misprices an order silently and invisibly. Exactly 27 entries carry it, and storefront/tests/shop-pages.test.tsx pins all 27 by code and by name — do not add a 28th without an accession. It is deliberately NOT the EU VAT or customs territory: the ISO entries that are territories of a member state rather than member states (AX Åland, GF French Guiana, GP Guadeloupe, MQ Martinique, RE Réunion, YT Mayotte) are flagged false and are charged the non-EU rate. That is a judgment call reported to the operator, not an oversight.",
